-- title:
--   Minimal level: corner of H¹ is the anemic localisation
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}\,\mathcal O$, let $p\neq 2$ be a prime lying in the maximal ideal of $\mathcal O$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$ (a two-dimensional $k$-space with a $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$-action factoring through a finite extension of $\mathbb Q$) which is absolutely irreducible after base change to $\overline k$. Let $S_{\min}\subseteq S$ be finite sets of primes with $p\in S_{\min}$, such that for primes $q\neq p$ one has $q\in S_{\min}$ exactly when $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$, and such that for $q\in S_{\min}$, $q\neq p$, every inertia element above $q$ has characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$. Let $N\geq 1$ be squarefree, with all its prime divisors in $S$, divisible by every $q\in S_{\min}\setminus\{p\}$, with every prime divisor $q\neq p$ in $S_{\min}$, and such that if $p\mid N$ then $\bar\rho$, viewed as an adic representation, does not satisfy `IsFlatAt` at $p$; let $r$ be a natural number, and assume weight-two cusp forms of level $\Gamma_0(N)$ are spanned over $\mathbb C$ by forms with integral $q$-expansion coefficients. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18) to $k$ such that for every prime $\ell\nmid N$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a non-unit in $P$ and every $\sigma$ which is a Frobenius at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\theta(T_\ell)X+\ell$. Let $hc_0$ witness that the operators $T_\ell$ on $H^1(N,\top,\mathcal O)=\mathrm{Hom}(\Gamma_{\top}(N),\mathcal O)$ indexed by the generators of [`CuspForm.AuxLevel.Gen N r S`](def/CuspForm_AuxLevelHeckeModule.html#L11) (primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid N$, $\ell\neq r$) commute pairwise, let $hcomm$ witness that all operators $T_\ell$, $U_q$ and $\langle d\rangle$ of [`CohCarrier.opFamily N ⊤ S 𝒪`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L91) commute pairwise, and let $\bar\theta$ be any function from [`CohCarrier.Gen N S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) to $k$ with $\bar\theta(T_\ell)=\theta(T_\ell)$ for all primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid N$. Let $Sp$ be an idempotent splitting (finitely many complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$, maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$ exhausting the maximal ideals, with $e_i\in\mathfrak m_j$ iff $i\neq j$) of the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathcal O}(H^1(N,\top,\mathcal O))$ generated by these operators, let $i_0$ be an index, and let $\pi_k$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra homomorphism from the corner ring at $e_{i_0}$ to $k$ carrying the image of each operator to $\bar\theta$ of its generator. Then there is an $\mathcal O$-linear isomorphism $e$ from the corner submodule $e_{i_0}\cdot H^1(N,\top,\mathcal O)$ onto [`CuspForm.AuxLevel.baseML N r S 𝒪 θ hc₀`](def/CuspForm_AuxLevelHeckeModuleBase.html#L31), the localisation of $H^1(N,\top,\mathcal O)$ as a module over $\mathrm{MvPolynomial}(\mathrm{Gen}\,N\,r\,S,\mathcal O)$ at the complement of the prime $\mathfrak m_\theta$, such that $e(m)$ is the image of $m$ under the localisation map `toML` for every $m$ in the corner submodule.
--
--   This is the cohomological form of Proposition 4.7 of Darmon–Diamond–Taylor (Wiles's Proposition 2.15) at $\Sigma=\emptyset$, to the effect that at the minimal level the local corner of $H^1(\Gamma_0(N),\mathcal O)$ cut out by an idempotent of the full Hecke algebra is carried by the localisation map isomorphically onto the localisation at the anemic maximal ideal attached to $\bar\rho$, with the auxiliary prime $r$ excluded from the anemic generators. It is used by [`CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_isCornerRealization_and_linearEquiv_baseML_of_squarefree`](thm.html#CuspForm.heckeLocal.exists_isCornerRealization_and_linearEquiv_baseML_of_squarefree) at the foot of the level-raising ladder of the Taylor–Wiles argument.
